/target
tbuild/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb
__pycache__/
.env

# ⚠ SCRATCH GOES HERE, and this entry exists because for months it went into the repository ROOT
# instead — an investigation's instrument dumps, scraped pages and diffed response bodies landing
# beside Cargo.toml because nowhere was sanctioned. CLAUDE.md's "Conventions that will bite you if
# ignored" carries the rule, the measurement that prompted it, and why the cost is not disk; this
# comment deliberately does not restate the numbers, because a second copy of a measurement is the
# thing this repository keeps re-learning about.
#
# Root-anchored, so a crate's own tmp/ — should one ever be wanted — is untouched. Create it on
# demand; it is deliberately NOT tracked with a .gitkeep, because an ignored directory holding one
# tracked file is a contradiction the next reader has to work out.
/tmp/

# Vendored FXCM ForexConnect SDK (42MB proprietary binary) + rescued POC — durable, not committed.
# The FXCM FFI lives in vt-venues/src/fxcm/; its build.rs reads FCSDK_DIR (defaults here).
/vendor/

# Program binaries the project RUNS, installed into the project folder rather than built into the
# workspace: `bin/lightgbm/{lightgbm,PROVENANCE}`, `bin/jforex/jforex-bridge.jar`, and `bin/jre/`,
# a portable Temurin JRE. Ignored for the reason `scripts/build_lightgbm.sh` gives — a
# platform-specific binary in the history would be carried by every clone — while the PIN that
# reproduces it is committed. Root-anchored, so a crate's own `src/bin/` is untouched.
#
# ⚠ NO EXCEPTIONS, and the one this used to carry is worth remembering. The JForex sidecar jar was
# force-added past this pattern by a four-line re-include chain, because it was the only way a
# clone could obtain a jar at all — 43 MB in every clone's history, forever, to serve the few
# checkouts that trade Dukascopy. `.github/workflows/release.yml` now ATTACHES it (and the pinned
# LightGBM binary) to the Release, and `scripts/fetch_release_tools.sh` installs both here, so the
# exception has a replacement and is gone. Do not add another: a runtime artifact belongs in a
# release, and the drift gate that justified committing this one is now a REPRODUCIBILITY gate
# (`.github/workflows/jforex-bridge.yml`) which needs no committed copy to compare against.
/bin/

# graphify knowledge-graph output (generated artifact; rebuildable via /graphify)
/graphify-out/

# Benchmark bars DB — copied from the Python app's parquet cache by
# bench/export_python_db_to_rust.py; regenerable, ~17 MB binary, not committed.
/market_data/

# Claude Code local state + git worktrees (embedded repos) — never commit, except the shared hook
# guard (`.claude/hooks/`: the script and its test; each user wires it in `.claude/settings.local.json`)
# and the project skills (`.claude/skills/`).
/.claude/*
!/.claude/hooks/
!/.claude/skills/

# Runtime logs (vike-log writes <exe_dir>/logs or $VIKE_LOG_DIR; target/ already covers dev runs)
logs/

# Competitive-research notes (local reference; large OSS steal-list / scale-strategy dumps — not committed)
/docs/research/

# SDD controller scratch (task briefs/reports/ledger) — session-local, never repo content
.superpowers/

# Playwright MCP + TradingView reference screenshots (never commit)
.playwright-mcp/
.firecrawl/
/tv-*.png
tbuild/

# headless chart→PNG export harness output (crates/vike-chart/examples/export_png.rs)
/chart.png

# keyword-research scratch (DataForSEO intermediates); the durable output is content_plan.json
content/data/_*.json

# cheap_np parity reference cache — the 148 MB pre-joined Polymarket signals parquet exported from
# the latency box, its 12,639-entry Python reference, and the fixed-width binaries derived from them by
# a Python generator that was DELETED (see crates/vike-strategy/tests/cheap_np_parity.rs). Staged
# locally, never committed, and no longer re-derivable;
# the committed proof is the 60-window sample under that fixtures dir.
/.cheapnp_ref/

# studio_shot PNG captures (generated by `cargo run -p vike-studio --features png-export --example studio_shot`)
/studio-*.png

# the component gallery's output (crates/vike-ui-theme/examples/gallery.rs), one PNG per theme
/gallery-*.png

# `just qa-shots` output — the GUI contact sheet (scripts/qa_shots.sh). Ignored WHOLE, and that is
# the point rather than an oversight: it holds PNGs, per-capture logs, and a throwaway settings root
# the app writes state and logs into. Nothing in it is a baseline — a committed screenshot is
# something to rebaseline, and this check exists precisely so there is nothing to rebaseline.
/qa-shots/

# `just api-docs` output — the built rustdoc API reference (scripts/build_api_docs.sh). Ignored
# WHOLE, for the same reason as `/qa-shots/` above: it is a couple of hundred megabytes of generated
# HTML rebuilt from source on demand, and nothing in it is a baseline. It is also a PUBLICATION, and
# a publication that can be committed is one that can be published without passing the redaction and
# the forbidden-token refusal the build performs — the tree it is built from is the public mirror's,
# not this one's.
/api-docs/

# The project settings directory. IGNORED WHOLE, no exceptions — decision 0086 ("settings live only
# in the database", 2026-09-26): `<project>/settings/db/vike.db` is the only settings store, so there
# is no TOML left that could be committable, and no template beside one to adopt from.
#
# ⚠ **This used to un-ignore five TOMLs — the four `vike_config` files plus a `*.example.toml`
# template per file — on the argument that a risk ceiling, a deployment config or a template held no
# secret and a team might want one under review.** 0086 REVERSES that: `vike-cli config set` is the
# one writer of a settings row now, the change journal is the review trail, and a settings FILE
# anywhere is simply not read — `config check` names a leftover one as unread rather than this
# directory's ignore rule mattering to it. `secrets.env` and `state/` were always ignored; now
# everything is.
/settings/

# The project USER-CONTENT directory, created by `vike-cli init`. Ignored WHOLE, with no exceptions
# and no un-ignored sample — the opposite disposition to `settings/` above, for the opposite reason.
#
# Nothing in here belongs to this repository. It is the user's strategies, run profiles, saved
# results and notebooks; a checkout is just one more machine they might run `vike-cli init` on, and
# a developer's own experiments showing up in `git status` is noise at best and an accidental commit
# of somebody's trading ideas at worst.
#
# The shipped EXAMPLES are not an exception either, and that is the point of them being compiled
# into the binary (`crates/vike-cli/src/cmd/init/content.rs`) rather than copied from a directory
# here: they must exist on a box with no source tree at all, so this repo is not their home and
# committing a second copy of them would just be a copy that can drift.
/user_data/

# `vike-agent-eval run`'s work directory (crates/vike-agent-eval/src/main.rs's `--work-dir` default).
# Ignored WHOLE for the same reason as `/qa-shots/` above: it holds a throwaway project root per
# case — settings, state, logs, the daemon's stdout and the two `DUMMY-` node keys — and nothing in
# it is a baseline. The binary removes it on the success path, but a killed run (or `--keep-work`,
# which exists precisely to leave it behind) does not, and `.github/workflows/agent-eval.yml`
# checks out with `clean: false` into a persistent checkout.
/agent-eval-work/

# `just marketing-shots`'s RUN SCRATCH (scripts/marketing_shots.sh). ⚠ NOT ignored whole, which is
# the opposite disposition to `/qa-shots/` above and is deliberate: a QA sheet is a throwaway a
# person looks at once, while these PNGs are DELIVERABLES that a page in another repository links
# by filename — so whether a captured frame is committed here is a decision for whoever ships it,
# not something this file makes for them. `.trader/shots/manifest.json` (the shared contract, and
# the script's own INPUT) must stay tracked either way.
#
# What IS ignored is only the machinery a run leaves behind: a throwaway project root the app writes
# settings, state and logs into, the fetched hist store, the journal directory, the per-capture logs
# and the index. Same argument as `/qa-shots/` — nothing in that list is a baseline.
/.trader/shots/settings/
/.trader/shots/hist/
/.trader/shots/journal/
/.trader/shots/*.log
/.trader/shots/*.dbg
/.trader/shots/index.txt

# `cargo build --manifest-path` writes a `Cargo.lock` BESIDE the manifest, and the plugin loader's
# refusal fixtures are their own tiny crates outside this workspace — so running
# `crates/vike-strategy-plugin/tests/load_refusals.rs` leaves four untracked lockfiles in the
# checkout. They are not build outputs under `target/` (which this file already ignores), so nothing
# covered them.
#
# ⚠ **That litter reached a SHIPPED BINARY, which is why this is not tidiness.**
# `crates/vike-buildinfo/build.rs` stamps `GIT_DIRTY` from `git status --porcelain`, and that counts
# UNTRACKED files deliberately (its own comment: "a false `dirty` costs one `git status`, a false
# `clean` costs an unidentifiable binary on a trading box"). `.github/workflows/release.yml`'s build
# job checks out with `clean: false` to keep `target/` warm, so litter from a previous CI job on the
# same runner survives into the release build. MEASURED: v0.1.33 reported `clean`, v0.1.34 reported
# `dirty` — the deployed binary could no longer be tied to an exact tree.
#
# ⚠ NOT a blanket `Cargo.lock` rule: the root lockfile is TRACKED and a merge gate compares it. This
# names only the fixture crates' own locks, which no gate reads and which `--locked` never sees —
# `build_stale_fixtures` builds each fixture fresh by content hash.
crates/vike-strategy-plugin/tests/fixtures/*/Cargo.lock
